# RunicAI RLM Summarizer

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"},
  {:runic_ai, path: "~/wrk/runic_ai"}
])

Code.require_file("support/runic_ai_livebook.exs", __DIR__)
```

## What This Notebook Teaches

Recursive Language Model style execution is useful when the source material is
too large, too structured, or too important to paste into one prompt. The key
move is to keep long source material in an external environment and run bounded
model calls over slices.

RunicAI's RLM layer is inspired by:

* ReAct: https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.03629
* Recursive Language Models: https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.24601
* lambda-RLM: https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.20105
* lambda-RLM implementation: https://github.com/lambda-calculus-LLM/lambda-RLM
* Slate agent-systems report: https://randomlabs.ai/blog/slate
* Harness recursive-design discussion: https://theharness.blog/blog/recursive-by-design/

The short comparison:

| Approach           | What grows?                                        | What owns control?          | RunicAI lesson                      |
| ------------------ | -------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------- | ----------------------------------- |
| Direct long prompt | One model context                                  | Prompt instructions         | Simple, but hard to inspect         |
| RAG                | Retrieved snippets                                 | Retriever + prompt          | Useful, but query quality matters   |
| ReAct              | Thought/action transcript                          | Model loop                  | Great for local tool use            |
| RLM                | External environment + recursive calls             | Programmatic scaffold       | Good for long-context decomposition |
| lambda-RLM         | Typed combinators + bounded leaves                 | Verified functional runtime | Closest to RunicAI's bias           |
| RunicAI RLM        | Runic workflow over refs, slices, leaves, reducers | Typed Runic workflow        | Inspectable and composable          |

## Provider Setup

```elixir
setup = RunicAI.Livebook.Setup.inputs()
```

```elixir
model = RunicAI.Livebook.Setup.read_model!(setup)
llm_opts = RunicAI.Livebook.Setup.llm_opts(model, max_output_tokens: RunicAI.Livebook.Setup.read_max_output_tokens(setup))
```

## Register Source Material

The source lives in an RLM environment. The workflow carries refs and slices
until it intentionally materializes bounded text at the leaf boundary.

```elixir
alias RunicAI.RLM

source_text = """
RunicAI models AI behavior as Runic workflow graphs.

It provides contracts for messages, prompts, LLM requests and results, agent
state, tool calls, context bundles, guard decisions, waits, reviews, traces,
evaluations, decomposition tasks, runtime sessions, and recursive long-context
workflows.

The RLM summarizer keeps source material outside the root model context. It
splits source refs into slices, materializes bounded artifacts, maps each leaf
with deterministic or model-backed work, and reduces leaf results into a final
artifact with provenance.

This differs from direct prompting because the workflow owns chunking,
provenance, cost boundaries, and reduction. It differs from a raw recursive REPL
because the public surface is typed Runic data instead of arbitrary generated
control code.
"""

{:ok, environment} = RLM.init_environment()
{:ok, source_ref, environment} = RLM.put_source(environment, source_text, label: "RunicAI RLM overview")

source_ref
```

```elixir
{:ok, slice_set} = RLM.split(environment, source_ref, chunk_size: 420, overlap: 40)
{:ok, artifacts} = RLM.materialize(environment, slice_set)

Kino.DataTable.new(
  Enum.map(artifacts, fn artifact ->
    %{
      id: artifact.id,
      length: String.length(artifact.content),
      preview: String.slice(artifact.content, 0, 120)
    }
  end)
)
```

## Visualize The RLM Summarizer Workflow

```elixir
alias RunicAI.Recipes.RLMSummarizer

rlm_workflow =
  RLMSummarizer.new(
    chunk_size: 420,
    overlap: 40
  )

RunicAI.Livebook.WorkflowViz.tabs(rlm_workflow)
```

## Run A Model-Backed Leaf Mapper

Each leaf call sees one bounded artifact. The reducer then combines those leaf
outputs. This keeps model context small while retaining provenance.

```elixir
RunicAI.Livebook.Setup.require_openai_key!()

mapper = fn artifact ->
  prompt = """
  Summarize this source slice in two crisp bullets. Preserve any concrete
  RunicAI terms.

  SOURCE SLICE:
  #{artifact.content}
  """

  {:ok, result} = RunicAI.LLM.generate_text(model, prompt, llm_opts)
  RunicAI.LLM.Result.text(result)
end

reducer = fn leaf_results ->
  leaf_summaries =
    Enum.map_join(leaf_results, "\n\n", fn leaf ->
      "- #{leaf.id}\n#{leaf.output}"
    end)

  prompt = """
  Combine these slice summaries into one concise explanation of RunicAI RLM.
  Mention what the workflow owns and what the model owns.

  #{leaf_summaries}
  """

  {:ok, result} = RunicAI.LLM.generate_text(model, prompt, llm_opts)
  RunicAI.LLM.Result.text(result)
end

rlm_run =
  RLMSummarizer.react_until_resolution(
    [
      chunk_size: 420,
      overlap: 40,
      mapper: mapper,
      reducer: reducer
    ],
    %{environment: environment, source_ref: source_ref}
  )

RLMSummarizer.latest_output(rlm_run)
```

```elixir
Kino.Layout.tabs(
  Result: Kino.Tree.new(RLMSummarizer.latest_result(rlm_run)),
  "Raw productions": Kino.Tree.new(Runic.Workflow.raw_productions(rlm_run))
)
```

## What To Notice

The model never receives the whole source unless you choose that policy. RunicAI
tracks refs, slices, materialized artifacts, leaf results, and reductions as
normal workflow values. That gives you a place to budget, parallelize, replay,
audit, or swap in a deterministic mapper.
